<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<PackageManifest Version="2.0.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema/2011" xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema-design/2011">
  <Metadata>
    <Identity Id="SsmsDataAnalyzer.6f2b6e2a-6c2a-4e3a-9c9a-2f6b0c8a1a4d" Version="0.27.0" Language="en-US" Publisher="SsmsDataAnalyzer" />
    <DisplayName>SSMS Data Analyzer</DisplayName>
    <Description xml:space="preserve">Per-column data profiling for SQL Server tables: fill rate, exact distinct counts, last-fill date, min/max and flags, directly inside SQL Server Management Studio.</Description>
    <MoreInfo>https://github.com/</MoreInfo>
    <!--
      No <License> element yet: this scaffold has no real EULA, and README-BUILD.md (build
      instructions, not licensing terms) was a placeholder that also isn't packaged into the
      VSIX, which VSSDK1310 correctly rejected ("license... missing from the file list").
      Add a real license file under this project directory and include it as
      <Content Include="..." IncludeInVSIX="true"> before setting <License> again for release.
    -->
    <Icon>Resources\DataAnalyzer.ico</Icon>
    <Tags>SQL Server, SSMS, Data Profiling, Data Analysis, Distinct Count</Tags>
  </Metadata>

  <Installation>
    <!--
      Pinned to SSMS 22 only, per CONTRACT.md and PLAN.md. Verified against
      Extensions\Application\extension.vsixmanifest (SSMS's own self-declared
      InstallationTarget Id) inside the SSMS 22 IDE folder. Do not add a VS-generic
      InstallationTarget — this extension is SSMS-22-exclusive by design (no compatibility
      shims, no older-shell branches).

      LOWER BOUND RAISED TO 22.3 (v0.27.0, field report 2026-09-24): a user on SSMS 22.0 got
      an error when SSMS started. The cause was not diagnosed — the decision was to stop the
      install rather than debug a build nobody here has. So treat 22.3 as "the oldest build we
      are willing to support", NOT as "the oldest build that works": 22.0 is known bad, 22.10 is
      known good, and 22.1 through 22.9 are simply untested. If the startup error is ever
      diagnosed and fixed, this can go back down.

      Do not widen it without that diagnosis, and note the comparison trap if you ever change
      it: these are per-component versions, not decimals, so 22.10 is ABOVE 22.3, not below it.
    -->
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Ssms" Version="[22.3,)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
  </Installation>

  <Dependencies>
    <Dependency Id="Microsoft.Framework.NDP" DisplayName="Microsoft .NET Framework" d:Source="Manual" Version="[4.7.2,)" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Prerequisites>
    <Prerequisite Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.CoreEditor" Version="[16.1.28811.260,30.0)" DisplayName="Visual Studio core editor" />
  </Prerequisites>

  <Assets>
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.VsPackage" d:Source="Project" d:ProjectName="%CurrentProject%" Path="|%CurrentProject%;PkgdefProjectOutputGroup|" />
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.MefComponent" d:Source="Project" d:ProjectName="%CurrentProject%" Path="|%CurrentProject%|" />
  </Assets>
</PackageManifest>
